= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\mathcal R=k/(\mu m_H)$ for the specific gas constant, with the printed $m_H$ denoting the atomic mass unit, and $a_{\rm r}=4\sigma/c$ for the <radiation constant>. In the low-mass envelope the <enclosed mass> is approximately $M$ and the outward <luminosity> is approximately $L$. The <stellar hydrostatic equation>, <stellar radiative diffusion>, and <ideal gas> equation give
$$
\frac{dP}{dr}=-\frac{GM\rho}{r^2},\qquad
\frac{dT}{dr}=-\frac{3\kappa\rho L}{16\pi a_{\rm r}c r^2T^3},\qquad
P=\mathcal R\rho T.
$$
The <Kramers opacity law> becomes $\kappa=(\kappa_0/\mathcal R)PT^{-9/2}$. Dividing the two differential equations eliminates radius and <mass density>:
$$
P\frac{dP}{dT}=\frac{16\pi a_{\rm r}cGM\mathcal R}{3\kappa_0L}T^{15/2}.
$$
Integrating from a surface matching point $(P_s,T_s)$ gives
$$
P^2=P_s^2+\frac{64\pi a_{\rm r}cGM\mathcal R}{51\kappa_0L}
\left(T^{17/2}-T_s^{17/2}\right).
$$
Deep compared with the <photosphere>, the surface terms are negligible. The <radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope> therefore has
$$
\boxed{P(T)=\left(\frac{64\pi a_{\rm r}cGM\mathcal R}{51\kappa_0L}\right)^{1/2}T^{17/4},\qquad
\rho\propto T^{13/4}.}
$$
This is the leading envelope approximation used in the later parts, rather than an exact atmospheric boundary condition at $T_{\rm eff}$.

At the base, <temperature>, <mass density> and <pressure> match the isothermal degenerate core. Let $K_{\rm NR}=K_1\mu_e^{-5/3}$, where $\mu_e$ is the <mean molecular weight per electron>. Equating the gas and <electron degeneracy pressure> gives
$$
\mathcal R\rho_bT_c=K_{\rm NR}\rho_b^{5/3},\qquad
\boxed{\rho_b=\left(\frac{\mathcal RT_c}{K_{\rm NR}}\right)^{3/2}
=\mu_e^{5/2}\left(\frac{\mathcal R}{K_1}\right)^{3/2}T_c^{3/2}.}
$$
Hence $P_b=\mu_e^{5/2}\mathcal R^{5/2}K_1^{-3/2}T_c^{5/2}$. Substituting this into the envelope relation at $T=T_c$ yields
$$
\boxed{\frac LM=\frac{64\pi a_{\rm r}cG}{51\kappa_0}
\frac{K_1^3}{\mu_e^5\mathcal R^4}T_c^{7/2}
=\frac{256\pi\sigma G}{51\kappa_0}\frac{K_1^3}{\mu_e^5}
\left(\frac{\mu m_H}{k}\right)^4T_c^{7/2}.}
$$
The <matching of a white-dwarf envelope to a degenerate core> thus gives **$L/M\propto T_c^{7/2}$**, the envelope relation underlying <Mestel's cooling law>. It depends on <pressure> as well as <mass density> continuity at the interface; otherwise the supplied core equation would not determine the matching <mass density>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In the <radiative-zero white-dwarf envelope>, $dP/dT=(17/4)P/T=(17/4)\mathcal R\rho$. Substitute this derivative into the <stellar hydrostatic equation>:
$$
\frac{17}{4}\mathcal R\rho\frac{dT}{dr}=-\frac{GM\rho}{r^2},\qquad
\frac{dT}{dr}=-\frac4{17}\frac{GM}{\mathcal Rr^2}.
$$
Integrating from the idealized zero-temperature surface at $R$ gives
$$
\boxed{T(r)=\frac4{17}\frac{GM}{\mathcal R}\left(\frac1r-\frac1R\right)
=\frac4{17}\frac{\mu m_H}{k}GM\left(\frac1r-\frac1R\right).}
$$
This also gives $d\log T/d\log P=4/17$. A finite surface <temperature> adds $T_s$ to the integrated profile if the pure power-law <pressure> relation is retained locally. Near the actual <photosphere>, the integration constant in the <pressure> law also matters; the displayed expression is the radiative-zero limit implicit in the printed result and accurate in the deep envelope when $T_c\gg T_{\rm eff}$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply the preceding <temperature> profile at the envelope base. Introduce the dimensionless parameter
$$
q=\frac{17}{4}\frac{\mathcal RT_cR}{GM}.
$$
Then $R/r_b-1=q$, so the <geometrical thickness of a white-dwarf radiative envelope> is
$$
\boxed{r_b=\frac{R}{1+q},\qquad
\frac sR=\frac{q}{1+q}\simeq q,\qquad
s\simeq\frac{17}{4}\frac{\mathcal RT_cR^2}{GM}.}
$$
The smallness to be established is therefore $\mathcal RT_c\ll GM/R$, up to the numerical factor $17/4$.

That ordering follows from the cold, strongly electron-degenerate <white dwarf> model. Throughout the mass-bearing core, $kT_c\ll E_F$, where $E_F$ is the <Fermi energy>. For nonrelativistic <electron degeneracy pressure>,
$$
\frac{P_{\rm deg}}\rho=\frac{2E_F}{5\mu_em_H}.
$$
The <stellar hydrostatic equation> gives a characteristic core $P_{\rm deg}/\rho$ of order $GM/R$. Consequently
$$
\frac{\mathcal RT_c}{GM/R}\sim\frac{5\mu_e}{2\mu}\frac{kT_c}{E_F}\ll1
$$
for fixed composition with order-one molecular-weight ratios, and **$s\ll R$**. Equivalently, the matched base <mass density> is much lower than a characteristic core <mass density>, since $\rho_b/\rho_{\rm core}\sim[\mathcal RT_c/(GM/R)]^{3/2}$. The degeneracy ordering applies in the bulk core; at the matching point degeneracy is becoming marginal.

The inequalities $T_c\gg T_{\rm eff}$ and $kT_c\ll E_F$ express different facts. The first justifies ignoring surface-temperature terms, while the second makes the envelope geometrically thin. If the core were hot enough that $q$ were not small, the exact expression $s/R=q/(1+q)$ would not justify a thin-layer approximation.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Compare two cooling states of the same <white dwarf>, holding its mass, nearly temperature-independent degenerate radius and composition fixed. The <matching of a white-dwarf envelope to a degenerate core> gives $L\propto T_c^{7/2}$, while the <geometrical thickness of a white-dwarf radiative envelope> gives $s\propto T_c$ at leading thin-layer order. Eliminate $T_c$:
$$
\boxed{\frac{s_1}{s_2}=\frac{T_{c,1}}{T_{c,2}}
=\left(\frac{L_1}{L_2}\right)^{2/7}.}
$$
Thus **a <luminosity> drop makes the envelope thinner**, with $s_2/s_1=(L_2/L_1)^{2/7}$. This is the <white-dwarf envelope thickness-luminosity relation>. Retaining finite thickness in the radiative-zero model gives the correction
$$
\frac{s_1}{s_2}=\left(\frac{L_1}{L_2}\right)^{2/7}
\frac{1+q_2}{1+q_1},\qquad
q_1/q_2=(L_1/L_2)^{2/7},
$$
which reduces to the boxed result for $q_1,q_2\ll1$.

